    "journey_v2": {
      "type": "ha-about-journey",
      "blocks": {
        "m1": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_01.png",
            "swatch_w": 236,
            "title": "New York, then Paris",
            "body": "<p>My work in fashion started in New York City in the 90s as an assistant, first to a well-known costume designer, then to the owner of New York’s biggest fashion trade shows.</p><p>When I was hired as an assistant to a French movie star and moved to Paris, fashion became a language that I learned and loved to speak. From major fashion houses to movie sets amazing clothes became part of my everyday scene.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m2": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_02.png",
            "swatch_w": 212,
            "title": "A first fashion book",
            "body": "<p>In 2002, I spent a couple of months in Morocco shooting my first fashion book, styled with a mix of vintage finds, luxury pieces gifted to me in Paris, and streetwear from a trip to Japan.</p><p>It was a seminal project. I realised the narrative power of juxtaposing cultures through textiles and film.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m3": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_03.png",
            "swatch_w": 198,
            "title": "Character, through wardrobe",
            "body": "<p>In 2003, I became the lead costume designer for a French TV series. It was a huge job and I loved it.</p><p>We shot eight episodes concurrently with eight main characters. I loved developing those characters through wardrobe.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m4": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_04.png",
            "swatch_w": 222,
            "title": "Dubai, in the making",
            "body": "<p>In 2004, I moved to Dubai when it was defining itself on the global stage. We built sets for luxury hotels and restaurants when there were still so few.</p><p>We often shot four or five versions of commercials to reflect the nuances of wardrobe across the Middle East, North Africa and the GCC. I became a specialist in making every detail aspirational and culturally accurate.</p><p>I worked with an exceptional team of tailors from Varanasi, India. Their knowledge was a major influence.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m5": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_05.png",
            "swatch_w": 184,
            "title": "India changes everything",
            "body": "<p>In 2009, I moved to Mumbai. My work as a photographer and stylist took me all over India and across the region: Afghanistan, Pakistan, Sri Lanka and Nepal.</p><p>I started to understand the depth of handcraft in South Asian cultures. Histories of families, communities and regions; the riches of empires and Gandhi’s movement for Independence.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m6": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_06.png",
            "swatch_w": 182,
            "title": "From Addis Ababa to Tribeca",
            "body": "<p>My love for textiles and design as a language played a central role as I became a film director, making culture films around the world for clients such as Christie’s, Condé Nast and The New York Times.</p><p>In 2022, I made a film in Ethiopia about women skateboarders. I shot some of the most compelling scenes of my career as they skated through Addis Ababa in traditional handwoven white dresses.</p><p>The film premiered at Tribeca Film Festival in 2023.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m7": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_07.png",
            "swatch_w": 210,
            "title": "The film became a collection",
            "body": "<p>While researching my next film, on handwoven cloth, I spoke to monks and physicists about weaving as vibration, and to curators and anthropologists about cloth as the basis of human civilisation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m8": {
          "type": "moment",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/HA_ABOUT_DYE_08.png",
            "swatch_w": 204,
            "title": "Hartwick Atelier",
            "body": "<p>It has been a long and spectacular road to creating Hartwick Atelier, a fashion brand made by Masters of refined handcraft.</p><p>Every piece is finely tailored with handspun and handwoven fabrics. We design using heritage techniques and natural dyes, produced by hand with the rhythms of the sun, wind and rain.</p><p>This is an analogue love story that I am delighted and honoured to share with the world.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
